			"widgets_values":[
				"Global Metadata\nA deeply sad and melancholic Mandarin acoustic folk song about adult loneliness, emotional isolation, fading ideals, and the pain of never being truly understood. Approximately 56–60 BPM in slow straight 4/4. The emotional atmosphere is cold, empty, weary, depressed, intimate, and quietly hopeless.\n\nThe song should feel like a solitary person sitting beside a window late at night, playing guitar after another exhausting day. The sadness must remain present throughout the entire song. Do not introduce a warm, comforting, inspirational, or triumphant emotional turn. Even the final chorus should feel like fragile persistence inside continuing loneliness rather than hope or victory.\n\nUse a minor-key harmonic language with slow chord changes, descending bass notes, suspended chords, minor add9 colors, open-string dissonance, and unresolved cadences. The vocal melody should mostly descend or remain within a narrow range. Important phrases may rise briefly before falling back into the lower register. Avoid bright major-key resolution and uplifting melodic movement.\n\nThe acoustic guitar must account for almost the entire instrumental sound. Use one close steel-string acoustic guitar as the main instrument, with an optional second acoustic guitar entering very softly near the final chorus. Preserve finger noise, fret squeaks, string resonance, imperfect timing, breathing, and natural room ambience.\n\nDo not use piano, drums, percussion, bass guitar, violin, orchestral strings, flute, harmonica, accordion, synthesizers, electric guitar, distorted instruments, jazz harmony, blues phrasing, rock energy, cinematic percussion, or pop-ballad production. The result must sound like a sparse and sorrowful solo acoustic-guitar performance.\n\nVocal Details\nA Chinese male lead with a low, warm, slightly hoarse folk timbre. The voice should sound tired, lonely, emotionally suppressed, and close to breaking, but never theatrical. Use an intimate close-miked delivery, soft chest voice, audible breathing, restrained volume, minimal vibrato, and weak phrase endings.\n\nThe singer should sound like someone accustomed to hiding his feelings because previous attempts to explain himself were ignored or misunderstood. In the verses, use conversational melodic phrasing with small hesitations and quiet pauses. Selected words may sound almost spoken.\n\nIn the chorus, let the melody rise only slightly. Increase emotional tension through breath instability, vocal grain, and firmer articulation rather than loudness. Allow one subtle crack or trembling sustained note at the emotional peak. The final chorus should remain lonely and restrained, not powerful or triumphant.\n\nDo not use backing vocals, female harmony, choir, vocal doubling, shouting, belting, long melisma, decorative ad-libs, or excessive reverb. Use only a short, dark room reverb that keeps the vocal close and exposed.\n\nArrangement\nCreate a complete song of approximately 115–120 seconds. Perform every supplied lyric line exactly once. Do not repeat lyrics, improvise new words, add long humming, or extend instrumental passages.\n\n0–5 seconds, Intro:\nBegin with a solitary steel-string acoustic guitar. Play a slow minor-key fingerpicked pattern using alternating thumb bass, open strings, and a descending inner melody. Let the strings ring into one another with slight dissonance. Include audible fret movement and quiet room noise.\n\n5–34 seconds, Verse:\nThe male vocal enters over the same fingerpicked guitar pattern. Keep the arrangement completely solo: one voice and one guitar. Use low bass notes, sparse upper-string arpeggios, and occasional pauses after emotionally important phrases. Do not introduce any other instrument.\n\n34–47 seconds, Pre-Chorus:\nThe guitar moves into slightly fuller arpeggios while maintaining the slow tempo. Add deeper thumb-picked bass notes and suspended chord voicings. Increase tension through harmony and vocal breath, not volume.\n\n47–70 seconds, Chorus:\nChange from individual arpeggios to very soft, slow downward guitar brushing mixed with fingerpicking. Keep the guitar dark, close, and restrained. Use a descending chord progression beneath the chorus. The vocal becomes more exposed and emotionally fragile, but the performance must remain quiet.\n\n70–76 seconds, Instrumental:\nPlay a short solo acoustic-guitar variation of the opening motif. Use natural harmonics and a descending single-note line. Keep this passage under six seconds.\n\n76–96 seconds, Verse:\nReturn to sparse fingerpicking and low-register singing. Make the guitar pattern even emptier than before, leaving more space around the voice. Use occasional muted strings to suggest emotional exhaustion.\n\n96–107 seconds, Bridge:\nReduce the guitar to isolated bass notes and incomplete chords. After the lyric questioning whether the final dream should be extinguished, stop the guitar briefly and leave a short natural silence.\n\n107–118 seconds, Final Chorus:\nBring back the full fingerpicked pattern. Add an extremely quiet second acoustic guitar playing only long open-string harmonics in the background. Do not broaden the arrangement beyond these two guitars. The lead vocal reaches its emotional peak through one restrained trembling note, then falls back into the low register.\n\n118–120 seconds, Outro:\nReturn to one guitar. Deliver the final short lyrics quietly, then end with an unresolved minor add9 open-string chord. Let the string vibration and room ambience fade naturally.",
				"[Intro]\n[Instrumental]\n\n[Verse]\n凌晨的灯还亮着\n桌上放着多余的碗\n通讯录翻了几遍\n没有谁适合说晚安\n\n白天我学会回答\n把难过说成了习惯\n电梯装满陌生的人\n没有一双眼睛停站\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\n他们说我想得太多\n成熟就应该沉默\n可若连痛也不能诉说\n懂事又算什么\n\n[Chorus]\n我不是害怕一个人生活\n是怕说出心事也没人懂我\n理想像门后微弱的灯火\n照着我，却照不进这座城\n\n若孤独是成年人的轮廓\n为何每张笑脸都如此相同\n\n[Instrumental]\n\n[Verse]\n朋友散在各自的地图\n消息停在改天再聚\n母亲问我过得好不好\n我把叹息换成没问题\n\n租来的房间没有回声\n只有吉他认得我自己\n\n[Bridge]\n我也怀疑坚持的意义\n当热爱换不来安稳\n可若连最后的梦都关掉\n我还能凭什么醒来\n\n[Final Chorus]\n我不是害怕一个人生活\n只是太久没人真正懂我\n理想若是迟到的灯火\n我愿守到天亮以后\n\n哪怕世界从未听见我\n至少琴弦知道我来过\n\n[Outro]\n灯熄了\n梦还醒着",
				222,
				"fixed",
				90,
				1.7,
				50
			],
